/* ============================================================================
fair-game-price.epher -- the entry fee that makes a dice game fair

A game pays 10 when a die shows 6, 1 when it shows 5, and nothing
otherwise (money units -- any currency works). One roll costs an
entry fee, and the fair fee is the
expected payout: the game is fair when the player's expected net gain
(payout minus fee) is zero (Ross, "A First Course in Probability",
ch. 4).

  E[payout] = 10 * 1/6 + 1 * 1/6 + 0 * 4/6 = 11/6,

so the fair entry fee is 11/6 = 1.8333... Charging 2 instead gives
the house an edge of 1/6 per roll: over six rolls the house expects
to keep one whole unit.

Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
============================================================================ */

// ---- results ----
// The payout for each face: 10 for a 6, 1 for a 5, nothing else.
print("P(6) pays 10:", 1/6)
print("P(5) pays 1:", 1/6)
print("P(other) pays 0:", 4/6)

// The expected payout by the definition: sum of payout * probability,
// the exact fraction 11/6 = 1.8333... in the payouts' own units.
const payout = 10 * (1/6) + 1 * (1/6) + 0 * (4/6)
print("expected payout:", payout)
print("about 1.83 per roll on average")

// The fair fee equals the expected payout; at a fee of 2 the player is
// giving away 1/6 of a unit per roll on average.
print("fair entry fee: 11/6")
print("player loss per roll at a fee of 2:", 2 - 11/6)
/* ---- expected output ----
= P(6) pays 10: 1/6
= P(5) pays 1: 1/6
= P(other) pays 0: 2/3
= expected payout: 11/6
= about 1.83 per roll on average
= fair entry fee: 11/6
= player loss per roll at a fee of 2: 1/6
*/
